{
  "version": 2,
  "note": "Macronutrients per stated portion. Values are USDA FoodData Central standard-reference entries unless another source is named; each food carries its FDC identifier so the figure can be checked. Alcohol and caffeine contents are cited separately where they apply, because neither is a macronutrient and both are pharmacologically active — ethanol and caffeine are both modelled as drugs in src/data/drugs.json, and a drink is the route by which a body actually receives them.",
  "sourceRoot": "https://fdc.nal.usda.gov/fdc-app.html#/food-details/",
  "fieldNotes": {
    "solidFraction": "0 for a clear liquid, 1 for a dry solid. Drives the Elashoff gastric-emptying exponent in src/sim/systems/gi.ts: liquids leave the stomach far faster than solids, and that difference is why a glucose drink and a bowl of rice produce quite different curves from the same carbohydrate load.",
    "fibre_g": "Unabsorbed bulk. It slows carbohydrate absorption and adds volume that reaches the colon, which is what makes a whole food behave differently from its refined equivalent.",
    "glycaemicIndex": "Relative to glucose = 100, from the International Tables (Atkinson et al. 2021) where an entry exists. It changes the SHAPE of the glucose curve rather than the area under it: the same grams of carbohydrate absorbed faster gives a higher, earlier peak.",
    "alcohol_g": "Grams of ethanol. The GI model hands this to the ethanol pharmacokinetics, which are zero-order.",
    "caffeine_mg": "Milligrams of caffeine, handed to the caffeine pharmacokinetics the same way."
  },
  "sources": {
    "USDA": {
      "label": "US Department of Agriculture, Agricultural Research Service. FoodData Central. US Government public domain.",
      "url": "https://fdc.nal.usda.gov/"
    },
    "GI2021": {
      "label": "Atkinson FS, Brand-Miller JC, Foster-Powell K, Buyken AE, Goletzke J. International tables of glycemic index and glycemic load values 2021. Am J Clin Nutr 114(5):1625-1632, 2021.",
      "url": "https://doi.org/10.1093/ajcn/nqab233"
    },
    "NIAAA": {
      "label": "US National Institute on Alcohol Abuse and Alcoholism. What is a standard drink? A US standard drink contains 14 g of pure ethanol.",
      "url": "https://www.niaaa.nih.gov/alcohols-effects-health/overview-alcohol-consumption/what-standard-drink"
    },
    "WHO_ORS": {
      "label": "World Health Organization / UNICEF. Oral rehydration salts: production of the new ORS. WHO/FCH/CAH/06.1, 2006.",
      "url": "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/WHO-FCH-CAH-06.1"
    }
  },
